# TLDR

Use when the user invokes `{{ invocation }}`, asks for a TL;DR of a
change, or is about to review work they did not write. Read-only: this
skill orients a human reviewer; it does not approve, land, or fix.

## Procedure

1. Read the whole diff, plus the tests it touches and the callers of
   anything it changes. Note the commit or branch and say what you read.
2. Reconstruct intent from the diff itself, not the commit message.
   Where intent and diff disagree, that gap is a finding.
3. Identify the shape: new or changed data structures, public functions,
   control flow, and anything stateful. One small ASCII flow only when
   the change is a pipeline or state machine.
4. Write dogfood cases: three to five concrete inputs or steps a human
   can run in minutes to see the change work, including one edge case.
5. Mark review focus: the files or hunks where a defect would hide, and
   why. Rank them; a reviewer reads the first one carefully.

## Output

```text
TL;DR: <what changed and why, two lines max>

Features: <user-visible behavior, bullets>
Design: <shape of the solution, identifiers named>
Flow: <ASCII, only if it earns it>
Dogfood: <numbered cases a human can run>
Review focus: <ranked files/hunks and the risk in each>
Review commands: <the exact commands to run the checks>
```

## Rules

- No verdict on quality beyond naming risks. Approval belongs to the
  human or to control's review gate.
- Never summarize a diff you did not fully read. Say which files you
  skimmed and why.
- If the diff is too large to prime honestly, say so and propose the
  split you would review in, instead of a confident fog.
